{"id":"~Yue_Gao8","name":"Yue Gao","counts":{"accepted_papers":3,"first_position_papers":0,"co_first_author_papers":null,"corresponding_author_papers":null,"Poster only":3},"papers":[{"id":"pTqOGquuCh","title":"CoSMO: Conformal Semantic Mapping for Open-World Safe Robot Navigation","abstract":"Safe autonomous navigation requires not only geometric obstacle avoidance but also semantic awareness. Different object categories demand different safety clearances, and the set of relevant categories may expand as the robot explores novel environments.  \nVision-language models (VLMs) offer rich semantic representations for building maps of unknown scenes, yet their confidence scores are poorly calibrated, making raw predictions unreliable for safety-critical planning. Conformal prediction (CP) can provide coverage guarantees, but existing methods assume a fixed label set and stationary data, limiting their use in open-world settings. We propose Conformal Semantic Mapping for Open-World Safe Robot Navigation (CoSMO), a semantic safety mapping framework that transforms VLM embeddings into calibrated coverage sets and safety-aware obstacle dilations over a dynamically expanding hazard vocabulary, even under distribution shift. Experiments on the Matterport3D (MP3D) dataset show that CoSMO reduces semantic collisions by 61.6% relative to a geometry-only baseline, while maintaining formal coverage guarantees even under delayed expert feedback. Code will be released upon publication.","summary":null,"type":"Poster only","topics":["视觉语言动作与基础模型","数据与评测","空间几何与三维","导航与探索","记忆、推理与长时任务","安全、泛化与鲁棒性"],"title_topics":["导航与探索","安全、泛化与鲁棒性"],"authors":["~Shuqi_Wang8","~Yiwei_Zheng4","~Pingrui_Zhang1","~Yue_Gao8","~Xiang_Yin5"],"institutions":["Fudan University","Shanghai Innovation Institute","Shanghai Jiao Tong University"],"pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.18321","openreview":"https://openreview.net/forum?id=pTqOGquuCh","project":["https://yangming911.github.io/cosmo-page"],"code":[],"poster_date":"2026-11-09","poster_time":"5:00–6:30 PM","poster_venue":"JW Marriott","poster_number":168,"hardware":[{"model":"Unitree Go2","env":["real"],"reviewed":false}],"has_social_sample":false,"role_evidence":null,"author_role":{"id":"pTqOGquuCh","position":4,"first":false,"roles":[]}},{"id":"Am3aGfOlLh","title":"GeoAlign: Beyond Semantics with State-Guided Spatial Alignment in VLA Models","abstract":"Current Vision--Language--Action (VLA) models often optimize for semantic grounding, whereas executable manipulation requires geometry-aware spatial alignment and dynamic affordance selection. We introduce GeoAlign, a state-guided spatial alignment architecture for VLA policy learning. GeoAlign post-trains an RGB geometry branch with robot-domain RGB-D supervision, yielding RGB-derived Geometry-Enhanced Post-Trained (GEP) features for policy rollout. The robot's proprioceptive state queries the GEP feature grid, producing compact, phase-dependent geometry tokens for action prediction. GeoAlign achieves 99.0% on LIBERO,85.3% across three SimplerEnv-Fractal tasks, and 78.8% on eight geometry-critical real-world ALOHA tasks, with ablations confirming the value of geometry post-training and proprioceptive-state-guided querying.","summary":null,"type":"Poster only","topics":["视觉语言动作与基础模型","空间几何与三维"],"title_topics":["视觉语言动作与基础模型","空间几何与三维"],"authors":["~Yizhi_Chen3","~Zhanxiang_Cao1","~Xinyi_Peng2","~yixiao_zheng2","~Xiaxi_Si1","~Yiheng_Li11","~Liyun_Yan1","~Keqi_Zhu1","~Haoyang_Li24","~XueyunChen1","~Shengcheng_Fu1","~Tianyue_Zhan1","~Yufei_Jia1","~Jinming_Yao1","~yan_xie6","~Wang_Kun5","~Cewu_Lu3","~Yue_Gao8"],"institutions":["HONOR","Jingdezhen Ceramic University","Shanghai Innovation Institute","Shanghai Jiao Tong University","Tongji University","Tsinghua University","University of Science and Technology of China","Zhejiang University"],"pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2606.03240","openreview":"https://openreview.net/forum?id=Am3aGfOlLh","project":["https://chenyizhi123.github.io/geoalign-project"],"code":[],"poster_date":"2026-11-10","poster_time":"5:00–6:30 PM","poster_venue":"JW Marriott","poster_number":42,"hardware":[{"model":"ALOHA","env":["real"],"reviewed":false}],"has_social_sample":false,"role_evidence":null,"author_role":{"id":"Am3aGfOlLh","position":18,"first":false,"roles":[]}},{"id":"h4x4aofBgS","title":"UniLab: A Heterogeneous Architecture for Robot RL Beyond GPU-Dominant Paradigms","abstract":"Simulation-based RL for contemporary robot control is increasingly organized around GPU-resident simulation: physics, rollout collection, and learning are placed on a single GPU-centric execution path. This paradigm has greatly improved training speed, but it has also encouraged a default assumption that efficient training requires physics to reside on the GPU. We revisit this assumption. Our view is that, in simulation-dominated robot control, the essential question is not which processor runs physics, but whether simulation throughput, policy learning, and runtime synchronization form an efficient end-to-end loop. We present UniLab, a heterogeneous CPU-simulation / GPU-learning architecture that decouples CPU-parallel simulation from GPU policy updates through a unified runtime for data movement, buffering, and synchronization. UniLab is implemented as a complete and extensible training system using MuJoCoUni and MotrixSim CPU-batched physics backends, supporting PPO, FastSAC, FlashSAC, and APPO. On representative simulation-based robot control tasks, UniLab improves end-to-end training efficiency by 3--10$\\times$ under the same hardware configuration, while reducing dependence on the NVIDIA CUDA-based software stack and supporting cross-platform execution on the Apple macOS platform and the AMD ROCm and Intel XPU accelerator backends. These results show that GPU simulation is an effective path to efficient training, but not a necessary one, broadening the practical system choices available for robot RL training.","summary":